{
 "id": "2022-tawil-curved",
 "name": "Curved vs Angular Living Rooms",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the explicit rating tasks in Tawil, Ascone and Kühn (2022), Frontiers in Psychology, \"The contour effect\". Four photo-realistic living rooms in a 2 × 2 design: angular vs curved furniture and windows × modern vs classic style, using the authors' 20 renders (5 viewpoints per room). Task 1 (general appraisal): the door view of each room, in random order, rated on six 0–100 visual analogue scales shown one per screen: edginess, roundness, curiosity, novelty, order/structure and complexity. Task 2 (aesthetic and stress response): four blocks in random order (beauty, liking, restfulness, stress), each rating all 20 pictures in a fixed order on one 0–100 scale. Numbers are hidden on the scales, as in the original. Image keys match the columns of the authors' OSF data (e.g. r1a_9 = modern angular, viewpoint 9; r2c = classic curved), so new samples can be compared with the published 198-person dataset. About 15 minutes. Differences from the original study: (1) the implicit tasks (approach-avoidance, IAT, dot-probe, manikin) and the questionnaires (VAIAK, DASS-21, personality, housing) are left out; (2) English wording translated from the paper's German items (Supplementary Table 1); (3) renders resized from 5075 to 2000 px wide; (4) the slider handle appears only after the first touch, so no default answer is recorded. Stimuli: authors' renders from OSF mfpk2 (no licence stated), used with credit; the template will be removed if the authors object.",
